Negative control for invoices.download.int.test.ts (AC-17, FE-3031's mid-run
addition — invoice/credit-note PDF download).

Authored by the developer seat (`agent-seat-separation.companion.md`,
developer-authors-mutant / prover-verifies-blind) — it knows the exact line
it mutates; the prover applies this blind and reverts, never reading
`invoices.services.ts` to construct it.

Assertion it attacks: the SUBSTANCE claim the file header states plainly —
"the response is handled as a Blob — never treated as JSON, which a shape
assertion cannot see." A TypeScript declaration check on `downloadPdf`'s
`Promise<Blob>` return type cannot see this: `response.json()` still
typechecks against that signature (both are `Promise<unknown>`-shaped at the
call site until runtime), so only a REAL request/response round-trip proves
the read is handled as a blob and not parsed as JSON — exactly what these
tests exist to do (per the module's own `resolveClientId`/`isAddressable`
seam doc, "the same standard every other read in this module is held to").
The filename-derivation and `isAddressable`-gate claims are each already
covered by their own dedicated assertions in this same file and are
UNAFFECTED by this mutation (confirmed below) — this control isolates the
blob-vs-JSON claim specifically, the one claim a shape/declaration check
cannot see.

Mutation: `downloadPdf` (`invoices.services.ts`) parses the download
response as JSON instead of returning it as a Blob.

Verified by hand before filing (developer seat, source-read; the prover
verifies the RED blind, per `agent-seat-separation.companion.md`): applying
this mutation and running
`pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/headless test -- invoices.download.int.test.ts`
reddens exactly 2 of the file's 3 tests — the wire+blob+filename test and the
credit-note-same-reader test, both of which await `downloadPdf()` and then
assert on the Blob handed to the save utility. The synthetic PDF fixture body
(`%PDF-1.4 fixture-bytes-only`) is not valid JSON, so `response.json()`
throws before either test's assertions run — the test never reaches
`expect(blobArg).toBeInstanceOf(Blob)` because the read that was supposed to
hand it a Blob threw instead. The third test (the `isAddressable` gate,
"issues NO download request... when signed out") is UNAFFECTED — it never
reaches the response-handling line at all, since `isAddressable` short-
circuits before any `fetch` — confirming this control is narrow to the
blob/JSON claim and does not spuriously flip the addressability gate.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with
`git apply -R`.

